version: 1.4.0

## 1.4.0 — 2026-09-03

pave-init 2.4.4, 2.4.5, and 2.5.0 alignment. Lead-behavior and revision-record
changes; the packaged graph's meaning does not move.

Evolution contract rule 7 is inverted. A run paused on a defect in its own
graph now continues on the reviewed, landed successor from its last satisfied
gate: the lead re-pins run state to the new revision and bundle digest, records
the authorizing approval verbatim, and landed work stays landed. A gate the
successor adds becomes a backfill duty per landed item, discharged before those
items are promoted and never marked passed for them. Closing the run and
starting a linked successor run is now the narrow exception: only a successor
that changes what a recorded outcome meant. Rule 1, resume step 1, and the
run-state schema's `workflow_identity` description now say a landing mid-run
does not move a running instance by itself, and the run-time bar reads
"never change the pinned graph's meaning in place" instead of a blanket "never
at run time". The binding-revision lane is stated as never changing graph
meaning, so never moving node, edge, or check counts, in the lead skill and the
README appendix.
  - Field evidence, this plugin's own run: it needed one review node added
    mid-run and the contract's only path was close-the-run-and-start-a-
    successor, so the v1 revision was amended in place instead.

Changed — the revision record moves from `workflow-manifest.yaml` plus full
`history/vN/` copies to one live `workflow.pave.yaml`, one append-only
`revisions.yaml`, and one `history/v<N>.patch` per landing, managed only by
`scripts/record_revision.py` (`init`, `install`, `propose`, `land`, `pin`,
`verify`, `rollback`). The package ships `revisions.yaml` (entries 0-4, patches
under `history/`) and is itself a valid root; `install` seeds a project's
evolution root from it.
`scripts/freeze_revision.py`, `codex/init_evolution_workspace.py`, and the
packaged `workflow-manifest.yaml` are removed. An existing root created by the
1.3.x manifest scheme migrates once per README "Migrating an existing
evolution root". "Freeze" is now "land".

New — `skills/vllm-neuron-parity/hooks/graph_edit_guard.sh`, registered in
`hooks/hooks.json` (PreToolUse Edit|Write|MultiEdit): denies a direct edit of a live
`*.pave.yaml` or `revisions.yaml` when `revisions.yaml` sits beside it and no
`.landing` marker exists. Eight controls register, disclosed in the skill
description.

Changed — successors are drafted and reviewed by pave-init's pave-evolve seats
(`pave-init:workflow-updater`, `pave-init:update-reviewer`), a run-time
dependency on pave-init 2.5.0 or later, disclosed in the skill's compatibility
field and the README. The lead never drafts its own successor. The Evolution
contract section shrinks from nine restated rules to one-clause applications
of rules 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8, and 9 with pointers to pave-init's
`references/pave-revisions.md`, records the plan fields `landing: user` and
`usage_ledger: kept`, and replaces the v1 freeze how-to with install, verify,
and pin. Run start and every resume run `record_revision.py verify
--pinned-revision --pinned-digest`: exit 3 routes per rule 7, exit 4 per
rule 9.

Changed — the run-state schema's `active_revision` minimum moves from 1 to 0,
so a ledger seeded at entry 0 pins cleanly; a fresh install pins the ledger
head.

Changed — the Claude agents `agents/adversarial-reviewer.md` and
`agents/adjudicator.md` move from opus to fable under the reviewer-tier rule (a
reviewer or adjudicator seat binds at or above the producer whose artifact it
judges). A runtime-binding change recorded by this release, user-approved
2026-09-03; the ledger holds no `kind: binding` entry because model bindings
live in `agents/*.md`, outside the YAML that `record_revision.py` lands. The
Codex binding stays gpt-5.6-sol.

Changed — lead dispatch, from the first run's corrections. The lead skill
gains a "Harness" paragraph: the package runs on Claude Code as well as Codex,
and the Codex tool names map to `Agent`, `SendMessage`, `TaskStop`, and
`ListAgents`, with pins from `agents/*.md` frontmatter; the README installs on
either harness. The stage-6 loop nodes (`scope_next_increment`,
`realize_increment`, `record_changeset`) run on one retained doer thread per
campaign instead of one thread per item (revision 4 binds it); a seat is retired in the same turn as
the state write that closes it, then the live-agent list is checked; a change
to an open seat's inputs is relayed before its result is read; internal fan-out
tiers per task (top tier for judgment, middle for medium work, low for
mechanical evidence) instead of one tier for all; a recorded gate-2 approval
stands across re-entries under the conditions the graph's
`design_approved_by_user` check states from revision 3, and the lead skill
points at that check instead of restating it. The investigator
and implementer contracts take public facts from web search or a fetch, cited
by URL. `references/artifact-layout.md` pins one `approvals/DECISIONS.md` per
campaign and one findings record per review node (a dated section per round,
one line per finding); the reviewer contract points at it.
`tests/test_workflow_pave.py` now fails when a README or newest-VERSION count
phrase disagrees with the validator, and `tests/test_codex_port.py` requires
the Claude Code tool names beside the Codex ones.
  - Field evidence, this plugin's own run: the lead spawned a seat per
    one-line increment, retired none of twenty finished seats, ran every
    fan-out on the top tier, and re-presented gate 2 after bookkeeping
    re-entries.

The stop guard asks six questions instead of four, answered only where the lead
finds an issue, else "lgtm": the next practical step toward the approved goal
(a declared outcome and edge, or a graph change to propose), ceremony since the
last check, landed work no review has seen, re-gating a recorded approval or
usurping a user decision, routing state that lives only in context, idle seats.
A recurring ceremony is a graph defect: the lead pauses, records the evidence
in run state and the standing review record, and routes it to the pave-evolve
seats; the updater drafts, the reviewer passes, the lead lands it under
`landing: user` and continues on it. Never a draft by the lead, never an edit
of the live graph outside a landing.
  - Field evidence: 55 increments (57 commits) landed with zero implementation
    review, each building on the last, under a check whose only alignment
    question was which DECLARED action comes next -- every wasted lap was a
    declared edge.

Hook mechanics are byte-unchanged: the cooldown breaker (at most one stop in
three), marker-authoritative discovery, the two-half lead-session identity
gate, fail-open, and the stop_hook_active short-circuit. "Socratic check"
remains the anchor phrase.

The packaged graph is the ledger head, revision 4 (32 nodes, 95 edges, 15
checks). Revision 1 (`kind: graph`) lands the field root's gate-2 narrow
triage, deletion instead of archiving at design re-entry, and the per-batch
implementation review node with its check; revision 2 (`kind: binding`) lands
its lead-mechanical re-entry instruments. Both landed under the user's verbatim
"approve revision 1" (2026-09-03) after a rule-5 review. Revision 3
(`kind: graph`) lands the gate-2 material definition with bookkeeping findings
as lead edits, the standing-approval conditions on `design_approved_by_user`,
the `design_loop_within_bound` check on the four repair edges (eight rounds
without `design_sound`, or two consecutive rounds at least half
repair-introduced, route back to `review_campaign_design` for the user's
disposition), block-scoped design re-entry, set scoping of up to three
low-complexity items, and one checkout per item for a concurrent-eligible set.
Revision 4 (`kind: binding`) lands the instruments: one reviewer seat retained
across a design entry's rounds with a delta-scoped read after a block repair
and no seat at the bound re-entry, the lead settling `scope_next_increment`
and the record delta on a verified block diff, and serial-by-default
realization of a set with opt-in one seat per item. Both landed under the
user's "Approve revisions 3 + 4" (2026-09-03) after a rule-5 review (round 1
REVISE, round 2 PASS). A fresh install seeds the head, and a 1.3.x root
migrates per README.
Manifests, the marketplace pin, and the package tests align at 1.4.0. A run in
progress keeps its installed 1.3.1 until its session restarts on the re-synced
plugin.

## 1.3.1 — 2026-09-02

pave-init 2.4.2 alignment: living documents have a declared cap. Field
evidence from this plugin's own GLM campaign: the increment plan reached 2,455
lines and 872 KB over 46 design laps because nothing bounded it, and one repair
lap then cost half an hour of re-reading (reading taken before the plan's later
collapse; no peak snapshot survives on disk). `references/artifact-layout.md` §4.12
names the living documents — increment plan, design record, regression matrix
draft, per-target delta report, costing and backlog report, PR evidence
package — and caps each at 400 lines and 60 KB,
both; the write-once registration record, append-only records (kickoff,
approvals, the reviewer stream), and transcripts sit outside the cap and are
never shrunk. The rules that keep the cap: a landed increment collapses to one
ledger row (its full contract carried by the evidence pointer; a planned
increment's stays inline for the gate-2 completeness check), frozen values stay
in the registration record, count tables are script output with their recompute
command, no defensive prose, a re-entry brief names the sections the seat may
touch, and an over-cap document makes the next lap a deletion lap.
The P9 registration digest binds the registration record only — never a block
of the increment plan, which must stay free to shrink.
`scripts/measure_artifact.py` is the one size instrument (lines, bytes,
sections, narration markers, `--strict`); the adversarial reviewer reports each
living document's lines and bytes every round and treats over cap as a material
finding; the implementer carries the collapse rules for the plan and design
record, the investigator for the delta and costing reports.
`write-for-reader.sh` names an over-cap document with its size once per session
and file, past its throttle (`VLLM_NEURON_PARITY_CAP_LINES`,
`VLLM_NEURON_PARITY_CAP_BYTES`), and tells the writer to leave a record or
transcript alone.
The graph, manifest, and pinned revision are unchanged — no graph meaning
moved, so this is an in-place patch, not a successor revision. Manifests, the
marketplace pin, and the package tests align at 1.3.1. A run already in
progress keeps its installed 1.3.0 until its session restarts on the re-synced
plugin. The legacy lead copy `claude/skills/vllm-neuron-parity/SKILL.md` is
deleted: neither harness loaded it (Claude Code scans the root `skills/` only;
the Codex manifest points at `./skills/`), and it carried superseded prose.

## 1.3.0 — 2026-09-01

pave-init 2.4.0 alignment. Documents are written for the reader: the lead and
every role that persists a reader-facing artifact now carry the plain-english
duty (one lead sentence per entry, identifiers paired with their plain names,
checker output cited from its own file), and the adversarial reviewer treats an
illegible reader-facing artifact as a material finding. Working state (attempt,
lease, measurement, increment, index, intake-preflight records) is exempt. A
seventh hook, `skills/vllm-neuron-parity/hooks/write-for-reader.sh` (PostToolUse Write|Edit,
advisory, marker-gated, first `.md` write under `artifacts/` then every third per
session), re-presents the duty when prose leaves context. Briefs are rendered
views of the graph and run state, and every seat carries the artifact-wins rule
for briefed facts. Run-state length caps enter on the observing rung: the schema
declares `maxLength` on every per-entry free-text field (`notes` stays the one
escape hatch, counted by a 128 KB whole-file warning); the validator reports cap
overs and unresolvable recorded paths as warnings with the exit code unchanged,
so the three blocking guards (protected branches, compile cache, venv and /opt
writes) stay armed on the live run; caps become errors in the next
version once a run starts clean. The stdlib fallback validator now enforces
`maxLength` itself and still fails loudly on any keyword it cannot enforce.
`hooks/hooks.json` returns to the shipped source (removed on 2026-08-29 by user
decision, restored on 2026-09-01 by user decision) so a fresh install on either
harness registers the same seven controls. `index/snapshots/` leaves the layout
tree. Manifests, the marketplace pin, and the package tests align at 1.3.0.

## 1.2.0 — 2026-08-31

Re-entry economics (pave-init 2.3.0) applied per verified usage evidence from
run-20260827-glm-5.3-flash-port (154 traversals; 17 screen laps on an unmoved
pin, 19 preregistration laps on a never-opened registration, 5 near-verbatim
record rebuilds). Graph: narrow-findings triage at gate 2 (three drafter-scoped
repair edges, recurrence fails closed into the bounded full lap) and re-entry
instruments on screen_pin_and_progress, preregister_acceptance, and
assemble_design_record — unchanged inputs settle lead-mechanically with no
seat; every registered-value touch keeps the full value-level seat. Active
revision is 31 nodes / 92 edges / 13 checks; in-place amendments are ledgered
in the evolution root's binding-revisions.yaml. SKILL.md pinned-graph counts
made count-free; investigator, implementer, and adversarial-reviewer contracts
carry the matching dispatch expectations. Both plugin manifests aligned at
1.2.0 (previously skewed 1.0.0/1.1.0).

## 1.1.0 — 2026-08-27

Codex CLI runtime binding. Adds the validated `.codex-plugin` manifest,
schema-valid active-run-scoped plugin hooks, six native custom-agent TOML
contracts, an explicit project or user agent installer, a durable project-local
evolution workspace, dual-root Codex dispatch instructions, and focused
runtime-binding tests. The approved PAVE graph and packaged v0 seed are
unchanged. The original Claude lead and role sources remain as provenance
records.

## 1.0.0 — 2026-08-26

Initial delivery. The vllm-neuron-parity plugin: a lead workflow skill
that drives a 31-node PAVE graph (upstream delta scan → route costing →
ranked backlog → user-approved campaigns → CPU increments → Neuron
hardware bring-up → measurement vs GPU baseline → adjudication →
evidence-backed fork PRs), six registered role agents (investigator,
implementer, measurer, adjudicator, adversarial-reviewer, rederiver),
five enforcement hooks (protected branches, compile cache, venv//opt,
stop guard, state staleness), run-state schema + validators, the
evolving-tier revision workspace (v0 graph, manifest, freeze tool), and
four domain references (artifact layout, measurement pitfalls, patch
mechanisms, collision ranking).
